Same text (the reference's pieces and random sequence, built in prepare) and the same work per run: scan every record, take the decimals of fields 1, 3 and 5, add them in order, average. The reference parses with the simd_csv crate (SIMD) and std's parse::<f64>; this is a plain quote-aware byte scanner and a decimal reader (digits as an integer, divided by a power of ten: correctly rounded, the same double as parse::<f64> for these ten-digit decimals), so the Rust column is partly a library comparison. The decimals in the text are written from integers (round(n * 10^10 / 139968), exact because the numbers are multiples of 1/139968) instead of formatted with {:.10}. The checksum is built by the benchmark harness: the three averages are the same in every run, so the reference's per-run hashes are the harness's one hash of each average (7 decimals) times the number of runs, plus the hash of the text, which this port answers. The reference's Vec<Point> of every record is not kept (the sums are taken while scanning). Stores are checked and undo-logged.
